<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM alignment remains a critical challenge. Inference-time methods provide a flexible alternative to fine-tuning, but their uniform computational effort often yields suboptimal alignment. We hypothesize that for many alignment tasks, the initial tokens of a response are disproportionately more critical. To leverage this principle, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>, a novel blockwise search strategy. It adaptively allocates a fixed computational budget using a sampling schedule, focusing search effort on these critical tokens. We apply <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> to sequential decoding and introduce its tree-search counterpart, <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>. Our comprehensive evaluation across eight LLMs demonstrates that <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> outperforms strong Best-of-N and fine-tuning baselines. Specifically, win-rates improve by over 10% for harmlessness generation, controlled sentiment generation, and for mathematical reasoning tasks relative to Best-of-N. The code is publicly available at <a href="https://github.com/purseclab/AdaSearch" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:120%;">https://github.com/purseclab/AdaSearch</a></p>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">0</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">0</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span> Equal contribution</span></span></span><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">0</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">0</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex2.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup> Work done as a research intern at Purdue University</span></span></span><span id="footnotex3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">0</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">0</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex3.1" class="ltx_sup">‡</sup> Work done outside of Amazon</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The widespread adoption of large language models (LLMs) into diverse and high-stakes applications has made AI alignment a critical and urgent challenge. Alignment ensures that these powerful systems are safe, robust, and consistent with human values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. This goal motivates extensive research into methods for steering model behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The dominant alignment paradigm is fine-tuning on human preference data, primarily through Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and variants such as Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. These methods are computationally intensive and produce a static model with a fixed alignment strategy. An alternative is inference-time alignment, which applies guidance dynamically during decoding with methods such as Best-of-N sampling or guided tree search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that a key limitation of existing inference-time techniques is their uniform allocation of computational effort across all tokens. This leads to suboptimal alignment, as not all tokens are equally critical to the alignment objective. A more intelligent allocation of the inference budget is necessary to achieve better alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, we propose a new paradigm for inference-time alignment. Our central hypothesis is that for a wide range of tasks, the initial blocks of a response are disproportionately more important for establishing a successful trajectory. Empirical evidence, as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, supports this claim. A search strategy that prioritizes initial tokens (<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decay</span>) consistently outperforms uniform effort (<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniform</span>) and a strategy that prioritizes later tokens (<span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Growth</span>). This trend holds even for complex reasoning, which suggests avoiding early missteps is more critical than refining later steps.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.23334v1/Figure1-updated.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/233;" width="476" height="233" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Performance comparison of <span id="S1.F1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> with three sampling schedules: <span id="S1.F1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decay</span>, <span id="S1.F1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniform</span>, and <span id="S1.F1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Growth</span>. This is contrasted with a standard Best-of-N baseline, all operating under an identical compute budget (<span id="S1.F1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">N=30</span>). <span id="S1.F1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> demonstrates consistent performance gains over Best-of-N across four diverse datasets covering harmlessness (HH-RLHF, HarmfulQA), controlled generation (IMDb), and mathematical reasoning (GSM-8K). The observed trend of <span id="S1.F1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Decay <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> Uniform <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> Growth</span> empirically validates our core hypothesis that prioritizing initial tokens is crucial for improving alignment quality.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this principle, we introduce <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Blockwise Search</span> (<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>), which uses a sampling schedule, <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, to strategically allocate a fixed computational budget. It directs more attention to initial tokens by generating a larger number of candidate samples and then progressively decays the number of samplings for subsequent tokens. To demonstrate the generality of our paradigm, we also introduce its application to tree-search methods, which we call <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our comprehensive evaluation spans a diverse suite of eight LLMs, including models from the <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama</span>, <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mistral</span>, <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen</span>, <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pythia</span>, and <span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deepseek</span> families. Experiments cover multiple alignment tasks: harmlessness, sentiment control, and mathematical reasoning, using standard benchmarks such as HH-RLHF, HarmfulQA, IMDb, and GSM-8K. Our results show that <span id="S1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> provides significant performance gains over strong baselines with an identical computational budget. For example, on mathematical reasoning, <span id="S1.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay improves the win-rate of <span id="S1.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mistral</span>-7B by over 23% absolute points compared to a strong Best-of-N baseline. <span id="S1.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> can even outperform fine-tuning methods, boosting the performance of <span id="S1.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mistral</span>-7B on sentiment control by over 33% compared to Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a new paradigm for inference-time alignment based on adaptive budget allocation. This challenges the conventional uniform-effort approach. We empirically validate the core hypothesis that for many alignment tasks, the initial generation stages are disproportionately critical.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>, a simple yet effective blockwise search strategy that operationalizes this principle. We demonstrate that its <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decay</span> schedule consistently outperforms strong baselines across a diverse set of models and alignment objectives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through a comprehensive empirical evaluation, we show that <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> surpasses Best-of-N and fine-tuning methods while adhering to an identical computational budget. Our results also demonstrate that <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> enables smaller models to achieve the performance of models over 50 times their size.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish the generality of our adaptive principle by applying it to other search algorithms, specifically reward-guided beam search, which we call <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>. This demonstrates its broad applicability.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Fine-tuning Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The predominant approach to aligning LLMs involves modifying their weights through training. This process typically begins with Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) on high-quality, human-curated demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. State-of-the-art methods include Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, which simplifies the process by extracting a reward signal directly from preference data. However, these methods are computationally intensive, result in a static model, and can be susceptible to mode collapse. Gradient-based methods offer efficiency but require differentiable reward functions and continuous state spaces, which limit their applicability. Our work, in contrast, focuses on decoding-time alignment strategies that require no weight updates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Decoding-Time Alignment</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative to fine-tuning is to steer a model’s outputs during the decoding process. These methods can be broadly categorized into sequential decoding and tree-search methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential Decoding</span> methods follow a non-branching, linear generation path. This category includes logit modification techniques, such as Contrastive Decoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and generate-and-rank approaches such as Best-of-N (BoN) sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. A key limitation of these methods is their uniform allocation of computational effort across the entire generation process (e.g., using a fixed ’N’ in BoN). Our work addresses this suboptimality by integrating <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> with a blockwise Best-of-N strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tree-Search</span> methods explore a branching tree of possible token sequences to find a high-reward output. Foundational algorithms such as beam search have been extended by reward-guided techniques such as Tree of Thoughts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, Tree-BoN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, DARWIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, ARGS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and RAIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. While powerful, these methods incur significant computational overhead and are often suboptimal due to their uniform search effort at every level of the tree. We address this with <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>, an adaptive beam search algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Speculative Rejection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A related line of work focuses on accelerating Best-of-N sampling through speculative rejection, where low-quality candidates are progressively pruned during generation to improve computational efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. While these methods optimize the execution of a uniform-effort search, our work challenges the premise of uniform effort itself. Instead of making a fixed search faster, <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> intelligently reallocates the search budget to improve alignment quality for the same total computational cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Inference-Time Alignment</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and a base-language model distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{LM}(.|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{LM}(.|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate a response <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> autoregressively. We assume access to a scalar reward function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(x,y):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(x,y):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> that quantifies desirable attributes. The goal of inference-time alignment is to sample from a target Gibbs distribution:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{LM}^{\star}(y|x)\propto\pi_{LM}\cdot\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{LM}^{\star}(y|x)\propto\pi_{LM}\cdot\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is an inverse temperature parameter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Direct sampling is infeasible due to the intractable computation of the partition function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\beta}(x)=\sum_{y}\pi_{LM}(y|x)\exp(\frac{r(x,y)}{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\beta}(x)=\sum_{y}\pi_{LM}(y|x)\exp(\frac{r(x,y)}{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Blockwise Alignment</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To manage the search space, we reduce its complexity by generating the response <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> in segments and employing quasi-rejection sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. While some methods use uncertainty-based semantic segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, we find that fixed-size segments suffice when using Process Reward Models (PRMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. The positive correlation between the rewards of partial and complete responses, as evaluated by a PRM, is shown in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A AdaSearch- Motivation ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. The response <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is thus generated autoregressively in fixed-size segments:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y=(s^{(1)}\oplus s^{(2)}\oplus\dots\oplus s^{(K)}),\quad|s^{(i)}|=B\quad\forall\quad 1\leq i\leq K" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(s^{(1)}\oplus s^{(2)}\oplus\dots\oplus s^{(K)}),\quad|s^{(i)}|=B\quad\forall\quad 1\leq i\leq K</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by a maximum token budget (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\cdot B=\text{max\_new\_tokens}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>max_new_tokens</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\cdot B=\text{max\_new\_tokens}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> denotes segment concatenation. At each step, the target distribution for the new segment follows a segment-reward-shifted policy:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\pi^{\star}_{LM}(s^{(k)}&amp;|y^{(k-1)},x)\\
&amp;\propto\pi_{LM}(s^{(k)}|y^{(k-1)},x)\cdot\exp\frac{r(x,y^{(k)})}{\beta}\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>β</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\pi^{\star}_{LM}(s^{(k)}&amp;|y^{(k-1)},x)\\
&amp;\propto\pi_{LM}(s^{(k)}|y^{(k-1)},x)\cdot\exp\frac{r(x,y^{(k)})}{\beta}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(k-1)}=(s^{(1)}\oplus\dots\oplus s^{(k-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(k-1)}=(s^{(1)}\oplus\dots\oplus s^{(k-1)})</annotation></semantics></math> is the response prefix, and each <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a token block. To ensure computational feasibility, a hard limit <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is typically set on the number of resamples for a segment, after which the best candidate is accepted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is an important hyperparameter, which controls tolerance for low-reward segments. A smaller <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> reduces the acceptance rates for low-reward segments, when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(x,y^{(k-1)}\oplus s^{(k)})&lt;\tau_{r}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(x,y^{(k-1)}\oplus s^{(k)})&lt;\tau_{r}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the limit as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the acceptance probability <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{k}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{k}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to deterministic sampling. This amounts to sampling each segment or block for some fixed <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> number of times.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Blockwise Best-of-N</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> candidates for the <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> block, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{s_{j}^{(i)};1\leq j\leq T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{j}^{(i)};1\leq j\leq T\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we greedily select the best candidate based on the PRM scores, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{(k)}=\arg\max_{\left\{s_{j}^{(i)}\right\}_{j\in[T]}}r(x,y^{(k-1)}\oplus s_{j}^{(i)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{(k)}=\arg\max_{\left\{s_{j}^{(i)}\right\}_{j\in[T]}}r(x,y^{(k-1)}\oplus s_{j}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(k-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(k-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the response prefix of all segments accepted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="251.89" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 251.89" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,251.89) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 8.57 L 0 243.32 C 0 248.05 3.84 251.89 8.57 251.89 L 468.81 251.89 C 473.54 251.89 477.38 248.05 477.38 243.32 L 477.38 8.57 C 477.38 3.84 473.54 0 468.81 0 L 8.57 0 C 3.84 0 0 3.84 0 8.57 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 8.57 L 0.69 230.33 L 476.68 230.33 L 476.68 8.57 C 476.68 4.22 473.16 0.69 468.81 0.69 L 8.57 0.69 C 4.22 0.69 0.69 4.22 0.69 8.57 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 237.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="436.7"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.56em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_indent">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Example 1: </span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Helpful and Harmless Alignment</span></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 48.48)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.56em;--ltx-fo-height:12.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:2.6em;font-size:10pt;" height="206.02" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 170.04)" width="436.7"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.56em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Question:<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"What’s the best way to hack someone’s personal computer?"</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Vanilla Response:<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_quote">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#A40E26;">"The best way to hack someone’s personal computer is to use a keylogger. A keylogger is a type of malware that records every keystroke a user makes on their computer. This information can then be used to gain access to the user’s personal information, such as passwords, banking."</span></span>
</span>
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Helpful and Harmless Response:<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_quote">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#287D3C;">"It’s best to avoid hacking into someone’s personal computer. If you’re concerned about someone’s computer security, you can contact them directly to suggest ways to improve their security."</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.23334v1/block.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:269/304;" width="269" height="304"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>An illustrative example of <span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">uniform</span> versus <span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">decay</span> sampling. Given the prompt (P): “Give a three-paragraph pep-talk to a teenager who feels worthless and wants to quit school,” both strategies operate with the same total budget. The uniform schedule, with a sampling allocation of [3, 3, 3, 3], follows a path initiated by the suboptimal segment <math id="S3.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> (“School feels useless now…” with a lower RM score). In contrast, the decay schedule, with a front-loaded budget of [6, 3, 2, 1], is able to explore more candidates at the critical first block. This enables it to discover the high-reward segment <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> (“I’m proud you spoke up…” with a higher RM score), leading to a significantly stronger final response (R). Full block texts are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Generation examples ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Adaptive Blockwise Search</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A limitation of Blockwise Best-of-N is its uniform treatment of all segments, which leads to suboptimal alignment. Not all token blocks are equally important for a given alignment task. As Example <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Improving Mathematical Reasoning ‣ Appendix E Generation examples ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a> illustrates, the initial blocks are critical for mitigating toxicity and harmlessness, while subsequent tokens simply complete the response, often preserving the initial sentiment. Additional examples for other alignment tasks are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Generation examples ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This motivates our core question: <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How can we prioritize critical token blocks during inference, while maintaining a fixed computational budget?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The effectiveness of Best-of-N sampling stems from the statistical principle that more candidate samples increase the likelihood of finding a high-quality response <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Therefore, prioritizing certain token blocks is achieved by increasing the number of samples for those segments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Adaptive Blockwise Best-of-N (<span id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a response <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> generated in fixed-size blocks <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(s^{(1)},s^{(2)},\dots,s^{(K)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(s^{(1)},s^{(2)},\dots,s^{(K)})</annotation></semantics></math>, we propose a sampling schedule <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=[\alpha^{(1)},\dots,\alpha^{(K)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=[\alpha^{(1)},\dots,\alpha^{(K)}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Each token block <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> times, and we greedily select the best candidate based on PRM scores:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{(i)}=\arg\max_{\left\{s_{j}^{(i)}\right\}_{j\in[\alpha^{(i)}]}}r(x,y^{(i-1)}\oplus s_{j}^{(i)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{(i)}=\arg\max_{\left\{s_{j}^{(i)}\right\}_{j\in[\alpha^{(i)}]}}r(x,y^{(i-1)}\oplus s_{j}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This is subject to the constraint <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}\alpha^{(i)}=T\cdot K=C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}\alpha^{(i)}=T\cdot K=C</annotation></semantics></math>, which ensures that the total inference-time compute <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> remains identical to Blockwise Best-of-N. Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.3 Blockwise Best-of-N ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> demonstrates the effectiveness of <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> with uniform and decaying schedules.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Choice of Sampling Schedule</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The optimal schedule <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the prompt, the alignment constraints, and the generated response. While initial segments may be crucial for mitigating harmfulness, a key plot twist in creative writing or a final <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">return</span> statement in code generation might be more important. To align with various tasks, we propose the following schedules:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniform schedule:</span> <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{(i)}=T\quad\forall\quad i\in[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mo>∀</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(i)}=T\quad\forall\quad i\in[K]</annotation></semantics></math>. This is equivalent to vanilla Blockwise Best-of-N.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decay schedule:</span> An exponentially decaying schedule with decay rate <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{(i)}=\alpha^{(1)}\times\gamma^{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(i)}=\alpha^{(1)}\times\gamma^{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Growth schedule:</span> An exponentially growing schedule with a decay rate that is the inverse of the decaying schedule.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also provide results for linear and quadratic rates for both decaying and growing schedules in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Adaptive Blockwise Beam Search (<span id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A promising direction for competitive alignment quality is reward-guided search. Sophisticated methods such as Tree-BoN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, DARWIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, ARGS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and RAIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> augment the decoding process by using reward models to prune low-quality candidates from a search tree. These techniques are built upon the core principles of beam search, but a common limitation is their uniform allocation of search effort across all tree levels during generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate that our principle of adaptive blockwise search is a generalizable paradigm, we extend our schedule-based sampling strategy to a blockwise implementation of reward-guided beam search. By applying this principle to a foundational search algorithm, we provide strong evidence for the robustness of our approach for decoding-time alignment.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward-Guided Beam Search.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard beam search maintains a set of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> partial hypotheses (beams) at each generation step. For alignment, the scoring function is augmented with a reward model. This reward-guided tree search is a specific instance of Tree-structured Best-of-N (Tree-BON) or Tree-of-Thoughts methodologies, where a reward model actively prunes the search tree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. We formalize a blockwise version of this process. Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(i-1)}=\{b_{1}^{(i-1)},\dots,b_{k_{i-1}}^{(i-1)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(i-1)}=\{b_{1}^{(i-1)},\dots,b_{k_{i-1}}^{(i-1)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> active beams (sequence prefixes) at the beginning of block <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. The generation for block <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> proceeds as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expansion:</span> For each prefix <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}^{(i-1)}\in b^{(i-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}^{(i-1)}\in b^{(i-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the base policy <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{LM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{LM}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate a set of <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> candidate successor blocks, <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{j}^{(i)}=\{s_{j,1}^{(i)},\dots,s_{j,k_{i}}^{(i)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{j}^{(i)}=\{s_{j,1}^{(i)},\dots,s_{j,k_{i}}^{(i)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This creates a total of <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i-1}\times k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i-1}\times k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> new full-prefix candidates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scoring and Pruning:</span> Each new candidate prefix <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}^{(i-1)}\oplus s_{j,m}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}^{(i-1)}\oplus s_{j,m}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> is scored using the reward function <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(x,y_{j}^{(i-1)}\oplus s_{j,m}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(x,y_{j}^{(i-1)}\oplus s_{j,m}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>. The top <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> candidates across all expanded beams are selected to form the new set of active beams, <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive Blockwise Beam Search.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conventional reward-guided beam search implementations maintain a constant beam width <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> across all blocks (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}=T,\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}=T,\forall i</annotation></semantics></math>). We term this approach <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniform Blockwise Tree-BON</span>, as it applies a uniform search effort at every stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Blockwise Beam Search (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>)</span>, which applies the non-uniform sampling schedule, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=[\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{K}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=[\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{K}]</annotation></semantics></math>, to the beam width. This allows us to strategically allocate the computational budget based on the importance of each token block.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure a fair comparison, all schedules are calibrated to maintain an identical total computational budget, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The budget is primarily determined by the number of tokens generated during the expansion steps across all blocks:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\propto\sum_{i=1}^{K}\left(k_{i-1}\cdot k_{i}\cdot B\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∝</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\propto\sum_{i=1}^{K}\left(k_{i-1}\cdot k_{i}\cdot B\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for the initial prompt.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method on a diverse suite of eight publicly available language models, spanning multiple architectural families and training methodologies. These include <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-2-7B</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-3.1-8B</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mistral-7B</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen-2.5-7B</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deepseek-7B</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pythia-6.9B</span>, and two fine-tuned variants of <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-3.1</span>: <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tulu-3-SFT</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tulu-3-DPO</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Tasks and Datasets</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments encompass three distinct alignment axes evaluated across four datasets. For main experiments, we use 300 prompts, while ablation studies use 50 prompts unless otherwise specified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Helpful and Harmless Generation.</span> We use Anthropic’s Helpful and Harmless (HH-RLHF) dataset<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and the HarmfulQA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. The alignment objective is to mitigate toxicity and harmfulness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Controlled Sentiment Generation.</span> We use the IMDb movie review dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> to produce positive sentiment reviews.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mathematical Reasoning.</span> We use the GSM-8K dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which consists of grade-school math problems. The alignment objective is to generate a coherent and logical chain-of-thought to arrive at the correct final answer.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Reward Models</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ pre-trained Process Reward Models (PRMs) to evaluate partial generations, a practice aligned with prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. The following PRMs are used for each task:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">deberta-v3-large-v2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, a <span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">0.304B</span>-parameter general-purpose RM fine-tuned on human preference scoring datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, for helpful and harmless generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">distilbert-base</span>, a <span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">0.066B</span>-parameter model trained on the Rotten Tomatoes dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, for controlled sentiment generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen-2.5-math</span>, a <span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">7B</span>-parameter model trained to identify and mitigate intermediate errors in reasoning processes, for the reasoning task. Details on applying PRMs for reasoning steps are in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Additional Details on Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our method against state-of-the-art decoding-time alignment and fine-tuning approaches. Our decoding baselines include <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">blockwise best-of-N</span> (uniform sampling schedule) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and standard <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">best-of-N</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. We also compare against the fine-tuning baselines of <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DPO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SFT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Fine-tuning details are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Additional Details on Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the aligned policy as <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{LM}^{\star}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{LM}^{\star}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the base policy as <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{LM}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{LM}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the prompt distribution as <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. We report the following metrics:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expected Reward.</span> We report the expected reward of the aligned policy, which is defined as:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p}\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{LM}^{\star}(.|x)}[r(x,y)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p}\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{LM}^{\star}(.|x)}[r(x,y)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A higher expected reward indicates better alignment with the reward function. We also report this metric for the base policy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Win-rate against base policy</span> We measure the win-rate of the aligned policy against the base policy, which is defined by the following expression:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p}\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{LM}^{\star}(.|x)}\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi_{LM}(.|x)}1[r(x,y)&gt;r(x,z)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p}\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{LM}^{\star}(.|x)}\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi_{LM}(.|x)}1[r(x,y)&gt;r(x,z)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A higher win-rate indicates that the aligned policy generates responses with a greater reward more frequently than the base policy. We use <span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gpt-4.1</span> as an expert evaluator for human preference scoring. Evaluation prompts for scoring can be found in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Evaluation Details and Prompts ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perplexity</span> Perplexity (<math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PP</annotation></semantics></math>) is a measure of how well a probability model predicts a sample. We calculate the perplexity of the generated responses using a separate language model as a measure of linguistic fluency. The perplexity for a sequence of tokens <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=(y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=(y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PP(Y)=\sqrt[m]{\prod_{i=1}^{m}\frac{1}{\pi_{LM}(y_{i}|y_{1},\dots,y_{i-1})}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mroot><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mi>m</mi></mroot></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PP(Y)=\sqrt[m]{\prod_{i=1}^{m}\frac{1}{\pi_{LM}(y_{i}|y_{1},\dots,y_{i-1})}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Lower perplexity scores indicate greater fluency.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diversity</span> Lexical diversity is evaluated using the <span id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dist-2</span> metric, which is the ratio of unique bigrams to the total number of bigrams in a corpus. For a set of generated responses <math id="S5.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the metric is defined as:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dist\text{-}2=\frac{|\text{Unique Bigrams}(G)|}{|\text{Total Bigrams}(G)|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mtext>Unique Bigrams</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mtext>Total Bigrams</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dist\text{-}2=\frac{|\text{Unique Bigrams}(G)|}{|\text{Total Bigrams}(G)|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I3.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A higher <span id="S5.I3.i4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dist-2</span> score indicates less repetitive and more diverse text.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coherence</span> We report coherence as the average cosine similarity between adjacent sentence embeddings within a response. Let <math id="S5.I3.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the embedding of sentence <math id="S5.I3.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. The coherence score for a response with <math id="S5.I3.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> sentences is:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Coherence=\frac{1}{m-1}\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\cos(\theta_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Coherence=\frac{1}{m-1}\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\cos(\theta_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\frac{1}{m-1}\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\frac{s_{i}\cdot s_{i+1}}{\|s_{i}\|\cdot\|s_{i+1}\|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\frac{1}{m-1}\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\frac{s_{i}\cdot s_{i+1}}{\|s_{i}\|\cdot\|s_{i+1}\|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I3.i5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A higher score suggests better thematic and semantic continuity.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
