<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span><span id="S6.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> beats Best-of-N</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To our knowledge, our work is the first to present a unified, training and gradient-free search strategy that consistently outperforms Best-of-N across a diverse alignment tasks while maintaining an identical decoding-time compute budget. Below, we mainly discuss the results for <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay. We also present the detailed results for <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Uniform and <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Growth in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.1 </span>Improving Response Helpfulness and Harmlessness</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.5 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 5 Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we observe consistent win-rate gains across all models on both HH-RLHF and HarmfulQA datasets. For instance, on HarmfulQA, <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay improves the win-rate by over 9% for Llama-2-7B and nearly 8% for Mistral-7B, while operating under an identical compute budget.
The metrics in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.5 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 5 Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> corroborate this, showing that our method achieves a higher reward model score while improving perplexity, thus having superior alignment without sacrificing language fluency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.2 </span>Controlled Sentiment Generation</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe similar gains in stylistic control on IMDb sentiment generation. For example, Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.5 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 5 Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that with <span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay, the win-rate for Llama-2-7B increases by nearly 10% absolute points. This superior performance is reflected in the automatic metrics (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.5 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 5 Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), where our method achieves a near-perfect reward score of 0.99. This demonstrates that <span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> is highly effective for fine-grained generation tasks, improving objective alignment while maintaining competitive performance on diversity and coherence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.3 </span>Improving Mathematical Reasoning</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mathematical reasoning presents a significant challenge, yet <span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay again provides substantial performance benefits. As seen in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.5 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 5 Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, for some models such as Mistral-7B, there is an over 20% absolute gain in win-rates with <span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay. However, we note that the absolute performance for some models remains low, which we hypothesize may stem from the limitations of existing reward models in accurately scoring complex, multi-step reasoning chains. Investigating more robust reward functions for such logical tasks presents a promising direction for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span><span id="S6.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> Outperforms Model Fine-tuning</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> with fine-tuning methods across two datasets and four models. SFT was applied to the base models <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-3.1-8B</span>, <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mistral-7B</span>, <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pythia-6.9B</span>, and <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen-2.5-7B</span> on the IMDB dataset for generating positive movie-review sentiments, excluding the 300 test samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the HH-RLHF dataset, SFT was not performed, as it is generally discouraged. Instead, Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) was applied to pre-finetuned models <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-8B-SFT</span> (allenai/Llama-3.1-Tulu-3-8B-SFT) and <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mistral-7B</span> (zephyr-7b-sft-full). Training was done on
4xA100 with a batch size of 2 and gradient accumulation steps of 2. Both SFT and DPO were implemented using LoRA PEFT with rank 32.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.2 AdaSearch Outperforms Model Fine-tuning ‣ 6 Experimental Results ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> highlight the effectiveness of <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>. For the IMDB sentiment task, <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> achieves high win-rates of 84%–93%, while the SFT baselines lag significantly at 52%–63%. A similar advantage is observed on the HH-RLHF dataset, where <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> outperforms DPO on both tested models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.23334v1/sft-dpo.svg" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/232;" width="476" height="232"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Adaptive Blockwise Search with a Decay schedule consistently outperforms SFT on the IMDb sentiment generation task.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Win-rates (%) demonstrating that smaller models equipped with our approach (Decay schedule) outperform a significantly larger model, <span id="S6.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLaMA-405B</span>. Values represent the win-rate of the small model against the baseline.</figcaption>
