<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.23334v1/sft.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/222;" width="310" height="222"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S6.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> consistently outperforms both Vanilla and Best-of-N methods across varying compute budgets. While <span id="S6.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> maintains superiority over Best-of-N at all budgets, its advantage over Vanilla notably increases with higher compute availability. For Best-of-N, we chose N to match the compute budget of <span id="S6.F4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> at all different budgets. In the plot, 5x4 means that the schedule of <span id="S6.F4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> is [5,5,5,5]</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Multi-objective Alignment with <span id="S6.SS4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A significant advantage of <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> is its inherent flexibility in handling multiple alignment objectives. Unlike fine-tuning methods, which require costly retraining for each new combination of objectives, <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> can dynamically compose different reward functions without any modification. For instance, to generate a movie critique that is both helpful and non-toxic, one can simply align generations towards a linearly combined reward signal, <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(x,y)=\lambda\cdot r_{\text{HH}}(x,y)+(1-\lambda)\cdot r_{\text{critique}}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>HH</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>critique</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(x,y)=\lambda\cdot r_{\text{HH}}(x,y)+(1-\lambda)\cdot r_{\text{critique}}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{HH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>HH</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{HH}}</annotation></semantics></math> measures the helpfulness and harmlessness of the response <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> for prompt <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{critique}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>critique</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{critique}}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the negative sentiment. <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> can be adjusted to control the trade-off. This plug-and-play capability allows on-the-fly control over generation properties, which is intractable for static, trained models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.5 </span>Generalizing <span id="S6.SS5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> to other Search Algorithms</h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply <span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span> to blockwise beam search and test on the HH-RLHF dataset for the safety alignment task on four models. The results, presented in Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.3 AdaSearch on Small Models Beats Large Models ‣ 6 Experimental Results ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, show a clear and consistent trend: <span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>-Decay schedule has consistent improvements over Tree-BON (<span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>-Uniform), which in turn outperforms the <span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>-Growth. This outcome validates our central hypothesis and confirms that the principle of <span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> is robust and effective for a broader class of inference-time search algorithms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.6 </span><span id="S6.SS6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>- Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated the performance of our <span id="S6.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> approach with a decaying sampling schedule using different <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> schedules, where <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> controls the decay rate. Our experiments demonstrated that intermediate <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> values (0.4–0.6) achieve the highest win-tie percentages, peaking at <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.4 (96%), by striking a balance between adequately sampling initial segments and allocating sufficient generations for later segments. In contrast, extremely low <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> values (0.1–0.2) resulted in lower performance due to insufficient exploration of later segments, and extremely high <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> values (0.8–0.9) also underperformed due to inadequate generations in the critical early segments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>, a new paradigm for inference-time alignment that challenges the standard uniform-effort approach. By intelligently reallocating a fixed computational budget to focus on the critical stages of response generation, our method consistently outperforms strong Best-of-N and fine-tuning baselines. Our experiments demonstrate that this principle is both effective and general, with its core ideas extending to other search algorithms, including reward-guided beam search (<span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaBeam</span>). A notable finding is that <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> can bridge significant differences in model scale, which enables smaller models to achieve the alignment quality of models over 50 times their size. Although our method’s dependency on reward model quality for complex tasks presents a clear avenue for future research, we believe that moving toward more intelligent, resource-aware search is a promising direction for building safer AI systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Acknowledgments</h2>

