<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Controlled Sentiment Generation.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Improving Response Helpfulness and Harmlessness. ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> summarizes detailed results on the IMDB dataset for positive movie reviews generation task. The decay schedules consistently achieve the strongest results, with exponential decay often slightly outperforming the other decay schedules. For instance, <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-3.1-8B</span> improves from 85.29% (linear) and 92.28% (quadratic) to 92.34% with exponential decay, while <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-3.1-8B-SFT</span> sees a similar trend, reaching 94.29% with exponential decay. Gains are generally small, in the range of 0.5–2% over the next-best decay schedule, indicating that all three decay variants are strong but exponential is the most reliable.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Within the growth schedules, linear growth achieves the best performance in most cases, including <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-2-7B</span> (84.56%) and <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen-2.5-7B</span> (79.78%), though the margins over exponential or quadratic growth are typically below 1–2%. Notable exceptions appear for <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mistral-7B</span> and <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen-2.5-7B</span> where the uniform schedule outperforms all decay and growth schedules by very small margin <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 1\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these results demonstrate that <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay is consistently the most effective strategy for controlled sentiment generation, and the exponential decay schedule is a safe choice that provides small but reliable improvements across models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Improving Mathematical Reasoning.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Improving Response Helpfulness and Harmlessness. ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> presents detailed results for the GSM<span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>8K reasoning task. As expected, smaller and less capable models such as <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-2-7B</span>, <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PYTHIA-6.9B</span>, and <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deepseek-7B</span> exhibit relatively low win-rates, with averages of 39.89%, 19.11%, and 42.11%, respectively. These results reflect their limited capacity for multi-step reasoning, where early mistakes are difficult to recover from.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Stronger models show substantial improvements. For example, <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-3.1-8B-SFT</span> reaches 94.68% with exponential decay, representing an improvement of roughly 5% over its quadratic decay performance (89.67% average vs. 86.33%). Similarly, <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-3.1-8B-DPO</span> achieves 91.34% with exponential decay, outperforming its linear and quadratic variants by around 7–10%. <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen-2.5-7B</span> also benefits significantly, achieving 90.00% with exponential decay compared to 81.67% with quadratic decay.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, for <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deepseek-7B</span> and <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PYTHIA-6.9B</span>, the growth schedules provide the best results, although the margins are small. For example, <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deepseek-7B</span> reaches 44.33% with exponential growth, just 1.7% higher than its best decay average (42.11%), and <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PYTHIA-6.9B</span> achieves 23.67% with quadratic growth, improving slightly over its 19.11% decay average.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these results highlight that <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span> <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>Decay remains the most reliable strategy for strong reasoning models, with exponential decay consistently achieving the best or near<span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>best performance. In contrast, smaller models such as <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama-2-7B</span> and <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PYTHIA-6.9B</span> struggle on GSM<span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>8K due to their limited reasoning capacity, and only marginal improvements can be obtained with <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Comparison of win-rates (%) between Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and <span id="A4.T9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay on the IMDB dataset. <span id="A4.T9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AdaSearch</span>-Decay is applied to the base models, while SFT results are from the fine-tuned models.</figcaption>
